{
  "adjustments": [
    {
      "target": "u1",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Extend the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline to single-object tasks whose exact target is not yet acquired or whose required placement is still unmet. Anchor the rule in the Actor's available information: current observation plus the last two pre-action observation/action pairs. If those show an immediately checked, opened, or toggled candidate did not reveal the target, choose a different reachable candidate; prefer locations/types not represented among the just-checked locations, including opening closed cabinets/drawers and checking shelves, additional sinkbasins, toilets, garbagecans, and other reachable receptacles. If the recent pairs show a return to an already-checked location or repeated checking of the same group without progress, choose an admissible location absent from those pairs. Preserve the known-instance priority: when a known location still contains a needed target instance, choose that location rather than an unknown one.",
      "reason": "The scope extension to single-object tasks is supported by `P8`: during a clean-soapbar task, `P8:34` opens cabinet 4, but the run repeatedly returns to cabinet 1/2/3/4, countertop 1, sinkbasin 1, and bathtubbasin 1 (`P8:38`, `P8:40`, `P8:45`, `P8:47`, `P8:49`, `P8:50`) while failing to inspect shelf 1, sinkbasin 2, toilet, or garbagecan visible in `P8:initial`, ending at `P8:outcome` step_limit. This supports both broader single-object search and anti-repetition. The original wording 'have not yet checked in the current scene' and 'still-unvisited candidate' can demand tracking beyond the execution protocol's current-plus-last-two-pairs view, so I narrow the trigger to observable recent history and concrete admissible choices. Historical broad-coverage guidance in `M2` still accompanied cycling and step-limit failure at `S1_R02/Q8:50`, so repeating a global coverage command alone is insufficient; the last-two-pair formulation is the testable refinement. The final clause preserves the Parent's successful known-instance return rule and avoids blocking required post-acquisition cleaning/cooling/heating sequences.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's needed object is still not found or not yet placed, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n-- If recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n-- Cover candidate container and surface types you have not yet checked in the current scene, including opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves and other receptacles, rather than cycling through the same small subset of locations.\n-- If you detect you have returned to an already-checked location without new progress, break the loop by going to a still-unvisited candidate location.\n+- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's exact needed object has not been acquired or its required placement is still unmet, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n+- Use the current observation and the last two pre-action observation/action pairs: if they show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different reachable candidate location instead of returning to that one.\n+- Prefer candidate container and surface types that are not represented among the locations just checked, including opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves, additional sinkbasins, toilets, garbagecans, and other reachable receptacles, rather than cycling through the same small subset.\n+- If the last two pairs show you have returned to an already-checked location or repeatedly checked the same group without new progress, break the loop by choosing an admissible location not present in those pairs; if a known location still contains a needed target instance, choose that known location instead of an unknown one.\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "target": "u2",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Strengthen exact-type selection: do not take or treat a similar wrong-type object as the target. Before cleaning, transforming, or placing an object as the target, confirm it matches the exact task-named type; if it does not, do not perform those target actions on it. If already holding a similar wrong-type object, return it to a reachable non-target surface or container or otherwise free your hands when an admissible action permits, then resume searching for the exact named type. If the task remains unmet after a placement and still requires an exact-type instance, do not repeat a placement of that same object or cycle the same locations; resume searching for the exact named target, including closed cabinets/drawers and shelves/other candidate surfaces, while keeping any already chosen valid target receptacle for subsequent instances.",
      "reason": "The exact-type direction is directly supported by `P7`: the task required a butterknife, but `P7:5` took knife 1, `P7:7` cleaned it, and `P7:9` placed it without reward; the run then cycled checked drawers and countertops and ended at `P7:outcome` step_limit. The original update says not to treat similar objects as the target but does not say what to do when the Actor has already taken one; adding 'do not take' and a recovery path for freeing hands makes the rule actionable. The resumed-search clause is coordinated with `u1` and with multi-instance behavior: after a wrong placement the Actor should search for the exact type rather than repeat that placement, while a valid already chosen target receptacle remains the placement target for remaining instances. Successful exact-object and clean-place behavior in `P6:3`, `P6:5`, and `P6:8`, plus the sink/fridge transformation sequences in `P9:5`-`P9:7` and `P10:12`-`P10:15`, are preserved.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Take and use the exact object type named in the task; if the visible objects are similar but of a different type (for example a knife where a butterknife is required), do not treat the wrong type as the target.\n-- Before cleaning, transforming, or placing the required object, confirm the object you are holding matches the exact type named in the task.\n-- If the task remains unmet after you place an object, do not repeat the same placement or cycle the same locations; resume the search for the exact named target object, including inside closed cabinets and drawers.\n+- Take and use the exact object type named in the task; if visible objects are similar but of a different type (for example a knife where a butterknife is required), do not take or treat the wrong type as the target.\n+- Before cleaning, transforming, or placing an object as the target, confirm the object you are holding matches the exact type named in the task; if it does not, do not perform those target actions on it. If you are already holding a similar wrong-type object, return it to a reachable non-target surface or container or otherwise free your hands when an admissible action permits, then resume searching for the exact named type.\n+- If the task remains unmet after you place an object and it still requires a needed instance of the exact named type, do not repeat a placement of that same object or cycle the same locations; resume the search for the exact named target object, including inside closed cabinets and drawers and on shelves and other candidate surfaces, and keep any already chosen valid target receptacle for subsequent instances.\n"
      }
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "update_ids": ["u1"],
      "question": "When the exact target is missing or its placement is unmet, does the Actor use the last two pre-action pairs to avoid immediate rechecks and choose a different reachable candidate type, including opening closed containers and checking shelves/basins, while still returning to a known needed instance and not delaying required post-acquisition transformations?"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "update_ids": ["u2"],
      "question": "Does the Actor avoid taking or using similar wrong-type objects such as a knife for a butterknife, and after a wrong or incomplete placement does it avoid repeating that placement and resume searching the exact named target in closed containers/shelves rather than cycling the same locations?"
    }
  ]
}
